====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To fit a cylinder liner.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1951|month=July|title=British Standard Locomotives|journal=Railway Magazine|page=444|text=They have a common chassis, except that the cylinders, 20 in. diameter in the Class "7", are '''linered''' down to 19½ in. in the Class "6".}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{af|en|line<pos:noun>|-er|id2=relational|pos2=relational suffix}} or {{af|en|-er|id1=measurement|pos1=measurement suffix}} (sense 5).

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A large [[passenger]]-[[carrying]] [[ship]], especially one on a regular [[route]]; an [[ocean liner]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1947|month=January and February|title=Notes and News: New Southern Channel Steamer|journal=Railway Magazine|page=49|text=With her luxurious furnishings and spacious accommodation the ''Invicta'', which is 350-ft. long and has a gross tonnage of 4,178, resembles a small '''liner'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=Foreword|passage=He turned back to the scene before him and the enormous new block of council dwellings. The design was some way after Corbusier but the block was built up on plinths and resembled an Atlantic '''liner''' swimming diagonally across the site.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A [[ship of the line]].
# {{lb|en|baseball}} A [[line drive]].
#: ''The [[liner]] glanced off the pitcher's foot.''
# {{lb|en|marketing|slang}} A basic [[salesperson]].
# {{lb|en|in combination}} Something with a specified number of lines.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=G. J. H. Van Gelder|title=Close Relationships|page=130
|passage=the following '''three-liner''' by an unknown poet}}
# {{lb|en|South Korean idol fandom}} person born in a certain year ({{m|en||XX '''liner'''}}); person who belongs to a certain [[line]]
#: {{ux|en|94 '''liner'''}}
# {{short for|en|penny-a-liner}}

